WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.942
Some ciphers swap letters for other letters. The Rail Fence cipher does something different. It keeps the same letters but mixes up their order. That is called a transposition cipher.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:25.529
Picture a fence with a few rails. You write the message in a zig-zag, down to the bottom rail and back up, one letter per column. Here is FENCE on three rails.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:36.926
Then read each rail from left to right, top rail first. Rail one gives F E, rail two gives E C, and rail three gives N. So FENCE becomes F E E C N.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:50.657
Draw as many rails as the key. Write the letters in a zig-zag, one per column. Read each rail left to right, from top to bottom. Then check the letter count matches.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:01:00.259
The cipher is weak because there are only a few sensible keys. An attacker can simply try each number of rails until the message makes sense.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:16.412
Here is a real question from Paper 2 in November 2020. Encrypt the word COMPUTATIONAL using a Rail Fence cipher with a key of four. Show your working. It is worth four marks.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:27.870
Draw four rails. C goes on rail one, O on rail two, M on rail three and P on rail four. Then bounce back up: U on rail three, T on rail two, A on rail one.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:35.664
Keep going. T on rail two, I on rail three, O on rail four, then N on rail three, A on rail two, and L on rail one.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:47.216
Now read along the rails. Rail one is C A L. Rail two is O T T A. Rail three is M U I N. Rail four is P O.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:02:00.619
Join the rails in order. The ciphertext is C A L O T T A M U I N P O. It has thirteen letters, the same as the plaintext. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:13.925
A common mistake is bouncing twice, writing two letters on the bottom rail in a row. Another is reading down the columns, which just gives back the plaintext.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:26.136
Now you try. Encrypt MEET AT NOON, written without spaces, using a Rail Fence cipher with three rails. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:41.729
Write the zig-zag on three rails. Rail one is M A O. Rail two is E T T O N. Rail three is E N. The ciphertext is M A O E T T O N E N, ten letters, the same as the plaintext.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:52.162
Remember the method: draw the rails, write a zig-zag with one letter per column, read each rail from the top, and check the count. Now try the practice questions for this type.
